nly actually reach a thin cross-section of that space: the plane parallel t=
o the RKHS passing through the initial function. And the RKHS itself depend=
s on the tangent kernel and therefore on the initial weights. If we identif=
y weight vectors and functions, then we can write this "feasible space" as =
w0 + RKHS(w0). And you claim that the (unique) element of w0 + RKHS(w0) tha=
t fits the data is also the closest point (in Euclidean norm) to w0 in the =
entire space that fits the data, is that right?</p></div></div><span class=
